# Rule Engine Troubleshooting Guide

## Common Issues and Solutions

### 1. Rule Index Mismatch in Daily Overtime Rules

#### Problem Description
Daily overtime rule resolvers fail to correctly identify the next rule in the sequence, causing incorrect threshold calculations.

#### Symptoms
- Daily overtime rules assign maximum threshold (24 hours) even when next rule exists
- Index out of bounds errors in rule resolvers
- Incorrect overtime calculations

#### Root Cause
The `ruleIndex` in `TimeRangeResolverContext` was based on the unified rules list (including system rules) but compared against only custom rules list size.

#### Solution
Use the `calculateCustomRuleIndex()` method in [BaseRuleEvaluator.java](mdc:src/main/java/io/recruitcrm/microservice/timesheet/rule_engine/rules/BaseRuleEvaluator.java):

```java
// Correct implementation
private int calculateCustomRuleIndex(IEvaluatableRule currentRule, List<IEvaluatableRule> unifiedRules) {
    if (currentRule instanceof CustomRule customRule) {
        List<CustomRule> customRules = unifiedRules.stream()
            .filter(CustomRule.class::isInstance)
            .map(CustomRule.class::cast)
            .toList();
            
        for (int i = 0; i < customRules.size(); i++) {
            if (customRules.get(i).equals(customRule)) {
                return i;
            }
        }
    }
    
    return -1; // System rules don't have a custom rule index
}
```

#### Files Affected
- [BaseRuleEvaluator.java](mdc:src/main/java/io/recruitcrm/microservice/timesheet/rule_engine/rules/BaseRuleEvaluator.java)
- [RangeBasedDailyOvertimeRuleRangeResolver.java](mdc:src/main/java/io/recruitcrm/microservice/timesheet/rule_engine/rules/timerange_resolver/range_based/RangeBasedDailyOvertimeRuleRangeResolver.java)
- [DurationBasedDailyOvertimeRuleTimeRangeResolver.java](mdc:src/main/java/io/recruitcrm/microservice/timesheet/rule_engine/rules/timerange_resolver/duration_based/DurationBasedDailyOvertimeRuleTimeRangeResolver.java)

### 2. System Rules vs Custom Rules Confusion

#### Problem Description
System rules (Regular Hours, Break) are treated as custom rules, causing index calculation errors.

#### Solution
Always check rule type before processing:

```java
// In resolvers, check if current rule is a custom rule
CustomRule currentRule = timeRangeResolverContext.getCurrentCustomRuleBeingEvaluated();
if (currentRule == null) {
    // Handle system rule case
    return TreeRangeSet.create();
}
```

### 3. Rule Precedence Issues

#### Problem Description
Rules are evaluated in wrong order, causing incorrect time range calculations.

#### Solution
Use [UnifiedRuleManager.java](mdc:src/main/java/io/recruitcrm/microservice/timesheet/rule_engine/utils/UnifiedRuleManager.java) for creating rule lists:

```java
List<IEvaluatableRule> unifiedRules = UnifiedRuleManager.createUnifiedRuleListWithSystemRules(
    customRules, getRegularHoursRuleType(), getBreakRuleType(), timesheet);
```

## Debugging Techniques

### 1. Rule Index Debugging
Add logging to verify correct rule index calculation:

```java
logger.logDebug("Unified rule index: " + ruleIndex);
logger.logDebug("Custom rule index: " + customRuleIndex);
logger.logDebug("Custom rules size: " + customRules.size());
```

### 2. Context Validation
Verify context creation in resolvers:

```java
// In resolver methods
if (timeRangeResolverContext.getCurrentRuleIndex() < 0) {
    logger.logWarn("Invalid rule index for custom rule");
    return TreeRangeSet.create();
}
```

### 3. Rule Type Verification
Check rule types during evaluation:

```java
if (currentRule != null) {
    logger.logDebug("Evaluating rule: " + currentRule.getRuleType() + 
                   ", isSystemRule: " + currentRule.isSystemRule());
}
```

